  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...After locally dense morning fog, temperatures\nwill climb above normal today through Friday.\n\nSurface high pressure is nearly overhead this morning. The\nresidual mid/upper level trough axis is starting to depart,\nwhich has allowed the more expansive stratus deck to steadily\nshrink. However, some low level moisture still exists, and some\nmore clouds have filled in across the central Shenandoah Valley\nand central Virginia. These clouds have been limiting fog\ndevelopment, but have seen river valley and ground fog in areas\nthat are clear. It's still a bit uncertain if fog can become\nmore widespread, but at least locally dense fog will be possible\nthrough sunrise.\n\nA shortwave ridge aloft will crest the area today while the\nsurface high starts moving off the coast. The ensuing southerly\nflow will result in temperatures rising above normal, with highs\nin the upper 70s and lower 80s expected for much of the area.\nStratocumulus clouds should scatter out through the day.\nHowever, a shortwave passing through the Great Lakes will bring\nincreasing high level clouds later this afternoon into tonight.\nThose clouds, combined with a light southwesterly flow, will\nmake any fog development much more localized tonight. Lows will\nbe in the upper 50s and lower 60s.\n\nAfter the departure of the Great Lakes shortwave Thursday\nmorning, a large ridge will build over the southeastern CONUS.\nTemperatures will rise further, with most of the lower\nelevations in the 80s on Thursday, and perhaps even nearing 90\nin a few spots. The ridge will break down on Friday as a trough\nmoves across the northern Great Lakes, but temperatures could\nsqueak a couple degrees higher. The exception will be west of\nI-81 where clouds will be thickening, but it will still be a\nvery warm day.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will bring rain showers and cooler \ntemperatures to the area this weekend.\n\nUpper-level troughing develops over the northern Plains before \ntracking east late in the week, with a stemming cold front forming \nahead of it. Infused with tropical moisture from the Southwest, this \nfront will move into the Mid-Atlantic between late Friday and \nSaturday before stalling to the south. Showers will become\nlikely along the Allegheny Mountains by Friday afternoon, but\nthere is some uncertainty how well the showers hold together\nfarther east moving into Friday night. As the front stalls,\nadditional rain showers will be possible throughout the \nweekend, although timing and total QPF remains uncertain as \nmodels deviate on where exactly the front will stall and the\ndevelopment of a wave of low pressure. Many of the 00Z models\nhave shown a decidedly southern shift to the frontal zone, as\nstrong high pressure crossing the Great Lakes exerts influence.\nBoth the ECMWF and GFS AI ensembles still show relatively high\nrain chances across the southern half of the CWA though, so the\ncoverage of rain is far from solidified. Regardless, \ntemperatures drop on Saturday in the wake of this front,\npotentially failing to reach 70 degrees depending on the amount\nof cloud cover.\n\nA wave of low pressure to the north and trailing secondary cold\nfront will push the stalled front offshore by early next week, \nwith high pressure building behind the front as upper-level \nzonal flow develops overhead. Conditions look to be much drier \nduring this period, with temperatures remaining cooler overall. \nBy Tuesday morning, temperatures over the mountains could \nreach the 30s with perhaps some frost potential in the coldest \nspots.",
